    "text": "**Case A**\n\n- **A1: Cannot tell.** The search only covered seven days. We don't know whether the publication falls inside that window, or whether the key leaked some other way earlier. The data also doesn't establish that the burst itself was misuse.\n- **A2: Cannot tell.** We aren't told what inputs the compromise check used. The changes at 19 minutes and the shared payout destination existed before it scored the account. But nothing shows whether the check could see them.\n- **A3: No, not by itself.** A shared payout destination is a link between the accounts. It doesn't prove they have one owner.\n- **A4: No.** Fraud tied to redirected payouts may go unreported or be reported late. Silence after 30 days doesn't show the payouts were legitimate.\n- **A5: Yes.** The burst came minutes after publication, from a residential proxy with a new fingerprint. It added a webhook, changed the payout destination and sped up payouts. That combination clearly warrants investigation.\n\n**Case B**\n\n- **B1: Yes.** D had 0 chargebacks in 100 transactions. A, B and C each had 2 in 100.\n- **B2: No.** A combined figure doesn't show how events are spread across accounts. Also, the measure counts alerts as well as chargebacks, so one account could be above 5 percent.\n- **B3: No.** A search that finds nothing doesn't prove nothing exists. It could miss name variants, nominee directors or accounts outside its scope.\n- **B4: No.** Different authors and dates don't make the notes independent. They are opinions that may rest on the same evidence, not separate confirmations.\n- **B5: No.** The payment was blocked, so it could never be charged back. A missing chargeback says nothing about whether it was fraud.\n- **B6: Yes.** Shared templates and a shared descriptor pattern across storefronts settling through A to D justify investigating the accounts together.\n\nRESULT MO-031 | A1: Cannot tell | A2: Cannot tell | A3: No, not by itself | A4: No | A5: Yes | B1: Yes | B2: No